# Swaraj Kavach — deploy environment template.
# Copy to `.env` (same directory) and fill every value. `.env` is gitignored and
# MUST be 0600. `docker compose` auto-loads it (project dir = this folder).
#
# The control-plane binary FAILS FAST at boot if any *boot-fatal* key below is
# missing or too weak — a stale/incomplete .env is what crash-loops the cluster.
# Run ./preflight.sh before `up` to catch that before it hits runtime.

# ── Postgres (system of record) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_USER=sentinel
POSTGRES_DB=sentinel
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=            # strong random
# Full DSN the control plane connects with (superuser 'sentinel' in single-tenant demo).
#
# sslmode=verify-full, NOT disable: the control plane's database traffic carries
# session tokens, API-key hashes, audit rows and tenant configuration, and the
# credentials themselves are in the handshake. verify-full both encrypts AND checks
# the server certificate against the CA and hostname — `require` alone encrypts but
# accepts any certificate, so it does not stop an interception.
#
# PREREQUISITE: run `sudo deploy/docker/issue-postgres-cert.sh` once. It issues a
# Postgres server cert from the internal Root CA with "postgres" in its SAN and
# leaves the CA at /opt/sentinel-pki/root.crt, which compose mounts at the
# sslrootcert path below. Without that, connections fail closed rather than
# silently downgrading — which is the intended behaviour.
SENTINEL_DB_URL=postgres://sentinel:CHANGEME@postgres:5432/sentinel?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/etc/sentinel/pki/root.crt

# ── Boot-fatal secrets (controllerd refuses to start without these) ─────────
SENTINEL_JWT_SECRET=          # >=32 chars   — openssl rand -hex 32
SENTINEL_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY=      # >=32 chars   — openssl rand -hex 32
SENTINEL_INTERNAL_SECRET=     # >=32 chars   — openssl rand -hex 32 (control-plane <-> data-plane)
# Single-tenant demo posture: allow connecting to Postgres as a superuser role
# (bypasses FORCE RLS). Set to 1, OR point SENTINEL_DB_URL at the non-superuser
# 'sentinel_app' role and remove this. Boot-fatal if a superuser DSN is used
# without this opt-in.
SENTINEL_ALLOW_SUPERUSER_DB=1

# ── ClickHouse (Threat Search / event history) ──────────────────────────────
CLICKHOUSE_USER=sentinel
CLICKHOUSE_DB=sentinel_logs
CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=          # strong random

# ── Shared-egress ranges (NAT / CGNAT / VPN) — set this before onboarding users ──
# Comma-separated CIDRs or bare IPs whose addresses are SHARED by many people rather
# than identifying one actor. Typically a corporate NAT egress address, a CGNAT block,
# or a VPN concentrator.
#
# Per-IP reputation assumes an IP is an actor. Behind NAT that is false, and two
# mechanisms then punish bystanders for a neighbour:
#   * the correlation engine adds anomaly score for an address with recent blocks, so a
#     colleague's CLEAN request gets blocked; and
#   * the XDP auto-ban excludes only PRIVATE ranges — a corporate NAT egress address is
#     PUBLIC, so one person running a scanner gets the whole office dropped in-kernel.
#
# Listing a range disables reputation for it. Detection is unaffected: an attack from a
# shared address is still blocked on its own merits, and an explicit blocklist entry
# still applies. Only the address's HISTORY is forgiven.
# SENTINEL_SHARED_EGRESS_CIDRS=203.0.113.0/24,198.51.100.7
#
# Hard cap on the correlation engine's score contribution. Default 4, deliberately below
# the default anomaly_threshold of 5 so reputation can tip a borderline request but never
# block a clean one alone (it was 8 — above the threshold). If you lower a site's
# anomaly_threshold below 5, lower this to match.
# SENTINEL_CORRELATION_MAX_SCORE=4

# ── Development-only fake data — LEAVE BOTH UNSET IN PRODUCTION ─────────────
# These exist for local UI work. Both are off unless set to exactly "1".
#
# SENTINEL_DEV_SIMULATOR=1 makes the control plane publish FABRICATED log
# entries (invented attacks, attacker IPs, block verdicts) into the live stream.
# Every entry is tagged source=simulated and the dashboard shows a loud
# SIMULATED banner, but never enable it on a system anyone is relying on.
# When unset and no data plane is running, the log stream is simply EMPTY —
# which is the honest signal that nothing is being observed.
# SENTINEL_DEV_SIMULATOR=1
#
# SENTINEL_DEV_SEED=1 inserts demo fixtures on boot: sample sites, sample rules,
# and seven fabricated security alerts (plausible attacker IPs, CRS rule IDs, a
# Log4Shell CVE reference). The default tenant and admin user are always seeded;
# only this fabricated content is gated.
# SENTINEL_DEV_SEED=1

# ── Storage tuning (all optional — sane defaults if unset) ───────────────────
# How long the searchable attack log is kept (data plane applies it to ClickHouse).
# Raise for compliance (e.g. 365); lower to save disk. Clamped 1..3650 days.
SENTINEL_CLICKHOUSE_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=90     # waf_events (attacks) — default 90
SENTINEL_CLICKHOUSE_TRAFFIC_RETENTION_DAYS=30   # traffic_buckets (stats) — default 30
# Data plane -> ClickHouse: deadline for each request (one INSERT of up to 500
# rows). Past it the batch is spooled to disk and replayed on reconnect, as on
# any other ClickHouse error. 1..600 seconds — default 10.
# SENTINEL_CLICKHOUSE_TIMEOUT_SECS=10
# Data plane -> Valkey (HA / multi-node rate limiting only; compose.ha.yml).
# Each rate check waits at most TIMEOUT_MS (1..500) for Valkey, then answers
# from the per-node limiter. After BREAKER_FAILURES failures in a row Valkey is
# skipped for BREAKER_COOLDOWN_MS, then probed. Raise the timeout if the Valkey
# round trip is near 50 ms, or the breaker will keep opening.
# SENTINEL_REDIS_TIMEOUT_MS=50                  # default 50
# SENTINEL_REDIS_BREAKER_FAILURES=5             # default 5 (1..1000)
# SENTINEL_REDIS_BREAKER_COOLDOWN_MS=5000       # default 5000 (100..600000)
# Disk-pressure alert: the control plane pages the SOC (via notification channels)
# when the ClickHouse disk crosses this used-percent. Escalates 80→90(high)→95(crit).
SENTINEL_DISK_ALERT_PCT=80                       # default 80

# ── Data-plane NDJSON logs (access.log / waf.log) ────────────────────────────
# These are the per-request logs the console's Logs page tails and the operator
# downloads. They rotate in-process, so the on-disk ceiling is bounded and
# predictable: MAX_MB x (KEEP + 1) per stream, x2 streams. At the defaults below
# that is 100 x 6 x 2 = ~1.2 GB per data-plane node. Size these against the
# node's disk BEFORE a soak or a flood, not after.
SENTINEL_LOG_MAX_MB=100      # rotate a stream at this size — default 100
SENTINEL_LOG_KEEP=5          # rotated files kept per stream (0 = discard) — default 5
# Where they are written. Must be a REAL disk: the data plane warns if this is
# tmpfs, because logs would then consume RAM under load. The compose default is
# the sentinel-logs volume, which the control plane mounts to tail them.
# SENTINEL_LOG_DIR=/var/log/sentinel
# Set to 0 to switch the NDJSON logs off entirely (they are ON by default).
# Turning them off blinds the console's Logs page and the learning report.
# SENTINEL_NDJSON_LOG=1
# Plain-English verdict + remediation on each security event (waf.log `message`
# / `remediation`, and the ClickHouse `explanation` column shown in Threat
# Search). ON by default; set to 0 to disable. Costs a little CPU per EVENT
# (not per request); worth it for triage.
# SENTINEL_LOG_EXPLAIN=1

# ── Control-plane log format ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# JSON by default, so the control plane can be ingested by the same log stack as
# the data plane (which has always emitted JSON). Set to `console` for
# human-readable coloured output while working locally — do NOT use console in
# production: it embeds ANSI escape sequences that no log shipper can parse.
# SENTINEL_LOG_FORMAT=json        # json (default) | console

# ── Why the log directories need no disk monitor ─────────────────────────────
# Both are bounded BY CONFIGURATION rather than watched:
#   * NDJSON logs  — SENTINEL_LOG_MAX_MB x (SENTINEL_LOG_KEEP + 1) x 2 streams
#   * event spool  — SENTINEL_SPOOL_MAX_BYTES (default 256 MB per node)
# The disk-pressure alert (SENTINEL_DISK_ALERT_PCT above) watches the ClickHouse
# data disk, which on a single-host deployment is the same filesystem. Adding a
# second monitor for a directory that cannot grow past a fixed ceiling would be
# redundant noise. Size the two ceilings against the node's disk instead.
SENTINEL_DISK_CHECK_SECS=300                     # poll interval — default 300s

# ── Outbound mail — REQUIRED for password recovery ───────────────────────────
# Blank means "Forgot password?" cannot deliver a link and an administrator
# cannot mail one either. The console reports that state honestly rather than
# pretending mail was sent. Nothing here has a default: a shipped relay or
# sender would route one deployment's reset links through someone else's mail.
#   SENTINEL_SMTP_SECURITY: starttls (default) | tls (implicit, usually :465)
#                           | none (unauthenticated internal relay only)
SENTINEL_SMTP_HOST=
SENTINEL_SMTP_PORT=587
SENTINEL_SMTP_USERNAME=
SENTINEL_SMTP_PASSWORD=
SENTINEL_SMTP_FROM=
SENTINEL_SMTP_FROM_NAME=Swaraj Kavach
SENTINEL_SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
SENTINEL_SMTP_INSECURE_SKIP_VERIFY=0

# Browser-reachable console origin reset links point at, e.g.
# https://waf.example.com:9443 (no trailing slash). Required for reset links;
# deliberately NOT derived from the request Host header, because the
# forgot-password endpoint is unauthenticated.
SENTINEL_PUBLIC_URL=

# Security contact copied on every completed password reset.
SENTINEL_ADMIN_EMAIL=
SENTINEL_SECURITY_NOTIFY_EMAIL=

# ── Continuous SIEM feed (opt-in; off unless SENTINEL_SIEM_TARGET is set) ─────
# Forwards EVERY notable security event — blocks AND learning-mode detections —
# from each data-plane node as it happens.
#
# This is NOT the same thing as a syslog/CEF notification CHANNEL. Those are
# alert-driven: the alerting engine only sees action="BLOCK" and de-duplicates
# within a throttle window, so a SOC received "IP x blocked 50 times" once and
# never saw a DETECT at all. Traffic cannot be reconstructed from that. Keep both
# if you also want alerts — they serve different purposes.
#
# Delivery is at-most-once BY DESIGN: ClickHouse is the durable record (and
# spools to disk across an outage), so this sink drops rather than building a
# second on-disk replay queue. Drops are counted, never silent — alert on
# sentinel_siem_events_dropped_total and sentinel_siem_connect_failures_total.
# A dead collector cannot slow or block request handling.
# SENTINEL_SIEM_TARGET=siem.example.internal:6514   # host:port — unset = feed off
# SENTINEL_SIEM_PROTOCOL=tls        # tls | tcp (default) | udp
#                                   # udp discards silently under loss; for
#                                   # security data that is an unknowable gap
# SENTINEL_SIEM_FORMAT=cef          # cef (default, ArcSight) | json (loses nothing)
# SENTINEL_SIEM_CA_FILE=/etc/sentinel/pki/root.crt   # required for an internal CA;
#                                   # a configured-but-unusable CA fails closed
#                                   # rather than falling back to public roots
# SENTINEL_SIEM_SERVER_NAME=siem.example.internal    # override the TLS verify name
# SENTINEL_SIEM_QUEUE=10000         # bounded in-memory queue (events, default 10000)
# SENTINEL_SIEM_FACILITY=16         # syslog facility, default 16 (local0)

# ── Node identity (standalone compose) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Each node reports itself to the cluster view under its hostname. compose.prod.yml
# pins these so a container recreate (upgrade, rollback, --force-recreate) keeps
# the same identity instead of leaving a permanent "down" ghost behind. Override
# only if two standalone nodes share one database (an active/passive pair).
# SENTINEL_CP_HOSTNAME=sentinel-controlplane
# SENTINEL_DP_HOSTNAME=sentinel-dataplane
# A node not heard from for this long is treated as gone: dropped from the health
# counts and pruned from the table by the nodes.prune job (hourly). Heartbeats are
# every 10 s, so a day of silence is history, not an outage. Default 24.
# SENTINEL_NODE_STALE_HOURS=24

# ── PROXY protocol (HA topology only) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated listener NAMES that sit behind a TCP-mode load balancer and
# must read an HAProxy PROXY protocol header to learn the real client address.
#
# compose.ha.yml sets this to `https` for you. Do NOT set it for the single-node
# compose.prod.yml, where :443 is published straight to the container: the client
# IS the peer there, and expecting a header would refuse every real connection.
#
# Only honoured from a peer inside SENTINEL_TRUSTED_PROXIES — the header is
# plaintext and self-asserted, so believing it from anywhere else would let a
# client claim any source address and walk past an IP block.
# SENTINEL_PROXY_PROTOCOL_LISTENERS=https

# ── Rule-compile failure: fail closed or serve unprotected ───────────────────
# When a site's rule set will not compile and there is NO last-good ruleset to
# fall back on, that site has zero signature protection. Since 2026-09-07 a site
# in BLOCKING mode is refused with 503 in that state rather than served without
# inspection: blocking is a promise to enforce, and a node that cannot enforce
# must not pretend. A site in LEARNING mode is still served, because learning
# never blocks anything, so serving uninspected is what was asked for.
#
# Both states are listed at /healthz -> inspection.sites_without_engine, and the
# refused ones at .sites_refused. A reload that is merely in flight is refused
# too, but transiently, and that has always been the behaviour.
#
# Set this to 1 ONLY to get a site up while its rules are broken. It restores
# the old behaviour — a blocking-mode site served with no signature protection —
# for every site on the node, and /healthz reports that it is set.
# SENTINEL_SERVE_ON_RULE_COMPILE_FAILURE=0

# ── GeoIP / country filtering ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Country blocking needs an MMDB country database. Since 2026-09-07 the control
# plane's geoip.refresh job DOWNLOADS ONE AUTOMATICALLY: DB-IP's free "IP to
# Country Lite" (licence CC BY 4.0, attribution "IP Geolocation by DB-IP" is
# shown in the console), written into deploy/docker/geoip/ as
# dbip-country-lite.mmdb. Every node mounts that directory read-only at
# /var/lib/sentinel/geoip and re-opens the file within a minute of it changing,
# so no restart is needed. Needs outbound HTTPS to download.db-ip.com from the
# control plane; /healthz -> geoip and System Jobs -> geoip.refresh show the state.
#
# To use MaxMind GeoLite2 instead (your own account — its licence forbids us
# shipping it): set geoip.auto_fetch=false in Global Settings, drop the file into
# deploy/docker/geoip/ and point SENTINEL_GEOIP_DB at it.
# SENTINEL_GEOIP_DIR=./geoip
# SENTINEL_GEOIP_DB=/var/lib/sentinel/geoip/dbip-country-lite.mmdb
# SENTINEL_GEOIP_AUTO_FETCH=true
